ue] [17592186431347 1077 "selection" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431347 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431331 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431347 1079 #uuid "d6c20d1b-8be1-5cba-93e4-c9f5ddddc294" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431347 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431348 1069 "q-126868" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431348 1071 "![](images/5e/5e11606627f4b91c977670cf9f919aff8b659cb5b4776cf3d04bb561b50b79fb.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1},$ ${C}_{2},$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431348 1072 #uuid "084d8c6c-71d5-5e51-afa2-e1398eb868b9" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431348 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/fa/fa1022d2a1718627898289abdd4d3035a6e06230eea16d0e46832675b2e37def.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{1}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+(-6)+(-8) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431342 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431349 1069 "q-126939" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431349 1071 "![](images/b1/b1a063fd261e790a8c3a56f741d0356f963300c3fbd88ddde03aa1744ef81053.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431349 1072 #uuid "420adaa9-fbd3-5686-bca6-a55b93dae680" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431349 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{4}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3}).$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}-(-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431338 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431350 1069 "q-126864" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431350 1071 "![](images/12/1253965689fbeb233ca3eda553cca8ea8bced9feb81ebc6565c9b65782066b47.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431350 1072 #uuid "4f71d9a8-f991-5b25-b40f-81e743cd06de" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431350 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/ca/cad0f92fdd888243562619b410a200ca040ca43f83bfd15fb124c8a8ecae4df6.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{2}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{2}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2-9+8 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431345 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431351 1069 "q-126855" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431351 1071 "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431351 1072 #uuid "5c72e07b-dd70-5ff7-8f10-0939e87dedc1" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431351 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431344 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431352 1069 "q-126891" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431352 1071 "![](images/e7/e71bc835596d8699688f3623f339c26d365a8bb6e888742cbea10fc9da722adc.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}5y\\,dx+11x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431352 1072 #uuid "8e6cffb2-428d-5a69-9a22-1115ee05f052" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431352 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=5y$ and $Q(x,y)=11x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=5,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(11-5)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =6\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =6\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {4}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 14\\pi  \\\\  & =84\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431341 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431353 1069 "q-126938" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431353 1071 "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431353 1072 #uuid "93158d64-29fd-580d-9bf5-f56c5e2adb43" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431353 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431339 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431354 1069 "q-126880" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431354 1071 "![](images/65/65047f9bf4f035000175fc55ee3ba6eeb4aa7ec39674a1984b95ba98a3f754f6.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431354 1072 #uuid "a2fd9b28-2de8-59c7-8e8c-46e14a79f680" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431354 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=8y$ and $Q(x,y)=13x.$ So,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=13.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8y\\,dx+13x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(13-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\,{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{6\\cdot 6}{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (18-4) \\\\  & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431346 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431355 1069 "q-126942" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431355 1071 "![](images/db/dbe6009d42623d3b3cd9f05380159bb0f4b8f22b920c1ef5d4d82b5dbf152d4a.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431355 1072 #uuid "d01c58ab-6639-5fe1-a3d3-01aeb78816a2" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431355 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}(x+1)$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y(x+1),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6xy+2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}{y}^{2}(x+1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}y+2xy)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6xy+2y)-2y(x+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}4xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(9-0)\\cdot (4-1)-(4-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431343 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431356 1069 "q-126946" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431356 1071 "![](images/9b/9be396fd57559bb77f92d5a0421f3a7cdca577203904e577da0caf5326caac91.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431356 1072 #uuid "f20c5355-cea7-5f07-9f8f-ed2bfe376f88" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431356 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x$ and $Q(x,y)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=8{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{2}{y}^{4}-x)\\,dx+(4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-8{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 2,-2\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-2}^{2}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}4{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))\\cdot (16-16)-(\\frac{1}{3}-0)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431340 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431357 1069 "q-126861" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431357 1071 "![](images/ef/efc1df0808af7ab4dacfb3031fdde2132bceaf0e89f88788110d7b5f5ed688bf.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-3,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431357 1072 #uuid "fa88e59c-b562-550a-9e35-b904fd919d3f" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true] [17592186431357 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/f7/f7869b25194e311c08df6c5b647fe9c53f7064c94eaa5d60fa1fa2551a6b58a1.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =7+(-3)+2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534659 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0918efff-3716-5e79-bc16-ce01b647dee2" 17592186431283 "answer-0a4ab89c-0bd1-5d9d-bbfe-405c2f326073" 17592186431284 "answer-0ca980d7-830a-5712-906f-d7004a38953f" 17592186431285 "answer-18f1a9de-bb0b-570a-b792-70641d11475f" 17592186431286 "answer-1d8f90a5-b39b-592d-a1d9-ab32a0cec7c6" 17592186431287 "answer-220be106-05bc-5093-b00a-7db60bd8ecaa" 17592186431288 "answer-236daf0a-8fec-5986-adef-4fec2089883e" 17592186431289 "answer-247e7c9b-86e2-5f51-8ba9-c9448bc0dc8e" 17592186431290 "answer-2980d347-4cd0-508a-a19e-609126e74ead" 17592186431291 "answer-2da41db7-6d0c-5183-9ea5-02fe84e79cce" 17592186431292 "answer-2f39220d-1633-509c-81a8-5131d70771c2" 17592186431293 "answer-30cf36fe-619f-5674-b7fc-e49a2947c716" 17592186431294 "answer-37256756-976d-5c9b-9621-726f21d7d740" 17592186431295 "answer-39f62df8-11d8-553d-8fdd-204067835d8d" 17592186431296 "answer-3add5703-5575-518a-8fb6-785fcc0ae348" 17592186431297 "answer-3b720f7a-85ac-5b6e-aa55-b3fe1bb64322" 17592186431298 "answer-3c2c4c9a-4ad0-5f0f-96f5-e727f1d26528" 17592186431299 "answer-3d37215b-1943-5905-97aa-565540294dc2" 17592186431300 "answer-408f7865-0008-58e6-85c6-ac75d60946d2" 17592186431301 "answer-4618a806-c076-56af-a407-64d1881a0e0d" 17592186431302 "answer-462f4f66-48fa-5a1b-a234-7ec355e80ccf" 17592186431303 "answer-4651760e-93e4-58a5-b28b-660b7c2991f7" 17592186431304 "answer-4724eac8-60ba-5daf-b39a-a8285610e36c" 17592186431305 "answer-51464593-b024-55b5-8ac6-2bf2b769ffdd" 17592186431306 "answer-59acde88-fd8f-54be-a537-e285b05463de" 17592186431307 "answer-5bef94c4-e6c8-5f82-b55a-2a267716a18a" 17592186431308 "answer-61820d09-1fb4-5a1a-a196-e88b05cc9b4b" 17592186431309 "answer-62afb4f8-e3ba-552d-b94f-0f8ee6e59d30" 17592186431310 "answer-64a00c0b-29bc-5b57-873f-d3261312d55f" 17592186431311 "answer-750e0059-fe60-559a-b87a-afcd74ddf718" 17592186431312 "answer-8173bde3-749c-53ab-95a4-d623b09672f7" 17592186431313 "answer-84acf7b5-dff2-5f83-9da4-34dd8d7b51ee" 17592186431314 "answer-8679841f-eada-5a6a-a61b-82efd37aeeeb" 17592186431315 "answer-88